  "summary": "Side-art missiles, backlit marquees and attract-mode loops — machines as billboards.",
  "description": "The golden-age arcade cabinet was a three-dimensional advertisement: hand-painted side art (Galaga's red bug fleet, Street Fighter II's full-body fighters), backlit marquees, bezel art framing the CRT, and control panels labeled like cockpits. Inside, attract modes looped demo gameplay to pull quarters; outside, cabinets lined arcades in color ranks. Cabinet art mixed airbrushed sci-fi realism, Katsuya Terada-style brushwork and graffiti energy, and its icons — Pac-Man mazes, Invader rows — escaped into streetwear and print. Modern barcades and re-releases (Arcade1Up, cabinets by Taito/Namco museums) keep the physical format alive as design heritage.",
  "culturalContext": "Arcade cabinets made machines into civic sculpture: the golden age defined youth public space (arcades as social third places), cabinet art and marquees trained commercial illustrators, and barcade culture plus museum preservation (International Arcade Museum) treat the machines as design heritage and neighborhood memory.",
